 "summary": "mcp-poison-scan is a CLI tool that inspects MCP server tool definitions for hidden instructions, invisible Unicode, shadowing and rug‑pull changes. The implementation works but suffers from high complexity, missing type hints and some unsafe exception handling.",
 "strengths": [
  "Clear separation into client, loader, rules and CLI modules",
  "Comprehensive heuristic rules for multiple poisoning techniques",
  "Good test coverage for core functionality"
 ],
 "top_fixes": [
  "Refactor the high‑complexity functions in cli.py and client.py into smaller helpers (reduce cyclomatic complexity below 10).",
  "Add proper type hints and docstrings to all public functions to satisfy static‑analysis standards.",
  "Validate and sanitize user‑provided inputs (e.g., --server command, URLs, headers) and avoid bare except blocks."
 ],
